const HQ_4=[
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Needham treats property rights as a 'policy variable' that can be changed to steer land use. Statement II: Needham follows Locke in treating property rights as natural rights that exist before the state recognises them.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 2, "e": "Statement I is Needham's own conclusion (pp. 28-29). Statement II is the opposite of his view: he cites Locke as the position he rejects and quotes Bromley that 'it is the fact that something is protected that makes it a right', so rights are created by the state, which is precisely why they can be a policy variable."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Needham argues that land-use planning is not really 'command and control', even though the term is often applied to it. Statement II: Under planning, most land-use decisions are still taken voluntarily by owners, and the authority has no right to take them unless it owns the land.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 0, "e": "Both are in the text (p. 24), and II is the reason for I: because owners keep the decision, planning can only restrict or influence it, which is why Needham concludes that planning 'will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions'. 'Both true, but no explanation' is tempting only if you read II as a separate observation, but Needham derives I directly from it."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "combo", "q": "Which claims are consistent with Needham (2012)? I. The central question for planning theory is whether land use should be decided by planning or by the market. II. Exercising a property right is itself an action 'in the market'. III. Shifting the protection of an interest from public law to private law amounts to creating a market in property rights.", "o": ["I and II only", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], "a": 1, "e": "II and III are Needham's own conclusions (p. 29): rights frame the exchange of interests in land, so changing who holds which right creates new exchange structures. I is exactly what he rejects: because the market can never be excluded (Lindblom), 'planning or the market?' is 'not useful' and only the mix is interesting."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: in the small Dutch housing project 'De Hoven', residents jointly own and maintain the green spaces between their plots. The municipality grants planning permission on condition that these spaces stay freely accessible to everyone and are properly maintained. Which situation from Needham does this match most precisely?", "o": ["A covenant scheme: buyers restrict their own plots and a residents' association sues those who break it", "A conservation agreement: a trust buys the development rights and never uses them, keeping land open", "An investor-landlord estate: one owner holds the rights and maintains the whole area to protect value", "Privately owned public space: rights created on purpose, with permit conditions on access and upkeep"], "a": 3, "e": "Needham's urban public spaces case (pp. 32-33) describes exactly this: small developments where residents own and maintain the spaces between plots, open to all, under planning conditions on access and maintenance. The covenant option is tempting because residents are involved, but covenants restrict what owners do on their own plots to protect amenity, and Needham notes such schemes are not binding in the Netherlands anyway."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: a Dutch business park is decaying. Firms own their buildings and refuse to fund shared upkeep, and tenants sign only short leases because they cannot sell the remaining term if they move. A consultant proposes a legal change so unexpired leases can be sold on, hoping an investor will then buy and run the park. In Needham's terms, the proposal is best described as...", "o": ["Coasean bargaining between the firms, which works without any change to the existing property rights", "public-law protection, in which the municipality enforces maintenance through stricter permit conditions", "changing a property right so that someone holds the rights needed to protect the firms' shared interest", "a selective incentive in Olson's sense, rewarding firms that contribute to the upkeep of shared spaces"], "a": 2, "e": "Needham's industrial-estate case (p. 32) says the market solution 'could be introduced into the Netherlands by changing a property right, namely by making it possible to sell unexpired rental leases', so that an investor-landlord holds the rights needed to protect the shared interest. Coasean bargaining is tempting, but the point is that bargaining fails here precisely because the current rights do not allow it; the rights themselves must change."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "critique", "q": "Commentary practice: which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Needham's proposal to create markets in property rights fits that perspective's logic?", "o": ["Marxist: markets in rights treat land as a commodity and tend to favour those who already own most property", "Sociological institutionalist: rights work well because actors' preferences are fixed before any institution", "Historical institutionalist: Needham overstates path dependence, since rights can change easily at any time", "Governmental model: markets are unnecessary because experts can determine the public interest objectively"], "a": 0, "e": "A Marxist reading (Foglesong, Madden and Marcuse) stresses the private ownership of socially produced land value and unequal ownership, so extending markets in rights looks like deepening commodification; Needham anticipates this by noting rights seem right-wing 'when rich and powerful people have more property rights'. The other pairings invert their perspective's logic: sociological institutionalists say institutions shape preferences, and historical institutionalists stress that rights change slowly. The governmental option fails too, as the model is a planning ideal rather than a critique of rights-markets."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "nuance", "q": "Which paraphrase states Needham's view on the limits of ownership most precisely?", "o": ["Ownership is absolute in principle, but public authorities may restrict it whenever the public interest requires them to do so", "Ownership is never absolute: owners may not harm others, though many countries allow harm if the benefit is greater", "Owners may never use land in any way that harms others, and every harm gives the neighbours an enforceable right to sue", "Ownership is limited only by public law; between private persons the owner's rights remain completely unrestricted"], "a": 1, "e": "Needham (pp. 26-27) says ownership is 'never absolute', the main restriction being that owners may not harm others, but adds (citing Coase) that most countries allow harmful use when its benefit exceeds the harm. The 'never harm' option overstates by dropping the Coasean qualification, and the 'only public law' option is wrong because he stresses that these restrictions operate between private persons, enforced on their own initiative."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "mechanism", "q": "Needham argues that a market in property rights is unrealistic for coherent regional development. Which causal chain captures his reasoning?", "o": ["Effects of one owner's choice fall on a few neighbours, so they can bargain directly like Coase, so regional plans become mostly unnecessary", "Effects of one owner's choice are spread over very many people, so each resident is given a right, so courts handle regional coordination", "Effects of one owner's choice are spread over very many people, so owners ignore them, so the state must expropriate all land in the whole region", "Effects of one owner's choice are spread thinly over many people, so none can realistically trade or enforce rights, so plans remain needed"], "a": 3, "e": "At the regional scale 'there can be so many people who are affected ... and the effects can be so small for each of those people separately' that a market in rights is not realistic (pp. 33-34), so land-use plans stay the main tool (with occasional private-law tools like conservation agreements). The third chain starts correctly but then contradicts the conclusion: giving everyone rights is exactly what would be ineffective, as with clean air."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "except", "q": "Which statement is NOT consistent with how Needham evaluates alternatives to regulation?", "o": ["He compares instruments by how effectively they reach the same, politically set goals (Williamson's remediability)", "He also asks what alternatives would mean for the use of scarce resources and for their distributional effects", "He assumes, as some economists do, that all public policy should ultimately aim at maximum economic efficiency", "He keeps the goals of land-use planning fixed and asks only whether other instruments could reach them as well"], "a": 2, "e": "Needham explicitly rejects the assumption 'that the goal of all public policy is maximum economic efficiency' (p. 30) and uses Williamson's remediability instead. The other three are accurate: he compares effectiveness for given goals and lists resource use and distributional effects among the questions (pp. 30-31). The remediability option may look suspicious because it sounds economic, but it is exactly his stated method."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "who", "q": "Which course author would most clearly endorse this claim: 'Some goals of land-use planning could be reached by deliberately turning interests into property rights, so that people's own exchanges realise the desired land use'?", "o": ["Moroni (2010), on an evolutionary theory of institutions", "Needham (2012), on interests and rights in property", "Taylor (2016), on rent-seeking at Melbourne's fringe", "Hindmoor (2006), on Olson's logic of collective action"], "a": 1, "e": "This is Needham's central question: whether planning goals can be realised by changing interests into rights so that 'markets in those rights would realize the desired land use' (p. 23). Moroni is the most tempting distractor because he also favours rules over end-state control, but he warns against designing institutions purposefully to reach specific outcomes and prefers general, evolved rules; Taylor shows insiders bending such rules."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: In Hindmoor's N-person prisoners' dilemma about joining a street protest, everyone ends up staying at home, although all would be better off if all joined. Statement II: Staying at home is the dominant strategy, because each person is better off defecting whatever the others do.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 0, "e": "Both are in the chapter (pp. 109-110): defection pays more whether others protest (4 > 3) or stay home (2 > 1), so the equilibrium is mutual defection even though all-cooperate is Pareto-superior. II is the reason for I; that is what makes it a dilemma rather than mere disagreement."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Olson defines public goods by both non-excludability and non-rivalry. Statement II: Olson's definition implies that common-pool goods such as fishing grounds face the collective action problem as well.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 3, "e": "Hindmoor stresses (Box 5.1) that Olson defines public goods 'solely in terms of their excludability', so Statement I is false. That is exactly why II holds: since rivalrous but non-excludable goods also fit Olson's definition, the problem 'affects the provision of both public goods and common goods'."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: According to Hindmoor, when participation itself is rewarding, the collective action problem 'simply dissolves'. Statement II: Like free legal representation by a union, process benefits can be obtained separately from the collective good.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 2, "e": "Statement I is Hindmoor's point (citing Hirschman, p. 117). Statement II reverses his distinction: free legal aid 'stands apart from' union membership and could be bought elsewhere, whereas process benefits are 'tied to' and even 'constitutive of' the collective good: the rush of a demonstration cannot be had without joining one."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "combo", "q": "Which statements about Olson's 'privileged group', as presented by Hindmoor, are correct? I. At least one member values the good so much that she will bear its entire cost. II. If several members are each willing to pay the whole cost, provision becomes certain. III. The other members can free-ride while the good is still provided.", "o": ["I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "a": 1, "e": "I and III define the privileged group (Olson 1965: 48-50; the flatmate who cleans alone). II is wrong: Olson recognises that with several such members the good 'may not be provided', because the situation becomes a game of chicken in which each waits for another to pay (pp. 115-116)."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: residents opposing the widening of a motorway near their Dutch town join blockades and marches for months. Interviews show many come for the energy of the crowd and the feeling of being part of a historic moment, which they say they could get nowhere else. Which concept from Hindmoor fits most precisely?", "o": ["A positive selective incentive: a reward that stands apart from the good, like free legal aid for members", "A privileged group: one member values the goal enough to bear the full cost of the campaign", "Process benefits: the reward lies in taking part itself, so it can't be had without joining in", "Conditional cooperation: each joins only as long as the others are seen to keep joining too"], "a": 2, "e": "This mirrors Chong's civil rights activists, driven by 'the adrenaline rush of direct involvement' and being part of history (pp. 117-118): the benefit is tied to participation, so the collective action problem dissolves. A positive selective incentive is the most tempting answer, and Hindmoor calls process benefits a form of it, but the defining difference is that a separable incentive like legal aid can be obtained apart from the collective good."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "mechanism", "q": "Hindmoor uses Ostrom's study of irrigation around Valencia. Which causal chain correctly explains why cooperation lasts there?", "o": ["The next user in the rotation gains from checking the current one, so monitoring is cheap, so conditional cooperation lasts", "State water inspectors check each user in the rotation, so monitoring is cheap, so coercion by a leviathan sustains cooperation", "The next user in the rotation gains from checking the current one, so monitoring is cheap, so the water is privatised to owners", "The next user in the rotation gains from checking the current one, so users turn altruistic, so no sanctions are ever needed"], "a": 0, "e": "Ostrom (1990: 95) shows the system is designed so that whoever is next 'has a built-in incentive to ensure that the person using it before them does not take more than their allotted share' (p. 121): cheap detection supports tit-for-tat conditional cooperation 'far from requiring the help of a leviathan or the market'. The state-inspector chain is the most tempting, but it replaces self-organised monitoring with exactly the leviathan Ostrom shows is unnecessary."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "critique", "q": "Commentary practice: which critique of Olson's logic of collective action would a sociological institutionalist most plausibly make?", "o": ["Collective action depends on class position in the relations of production, so free-riding is above all a symptom of capitalism", "Preferences and group identities are shaped by norms, so taking self-interest as given misses why people join groups", "Nobody can know enough to design selective incentives well, so cooperation is best left to an order that emerges spontaneously", "Groups stay locked into paths set at earlier critical junctures, so present incentives hardly matter for whether they cooperate"], "a": 1, "e": "Sociological institutionalism holds that institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences', so it questions Olson's given self-interest; Hindmoor himself concedes people contribute because norms say they 'ought to' and calls for work on group identity. The other options are recognisably Marxist, Hayekian and historical institutionalist critiques, so they do not fit the sociological logic."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "nuance", "q": "Hindmoor discusses thresholds of cooperation (Figure 5.5). Which paraphrase of his conclusion is most precise?", "o": ["Collective action succeeds only if most members set aside their self-interest, both while it starts and while it is sustained", "Once the threshold of cooperators is passed, cooperation is guaranteed to spread until every member of the group cooperates", "Unconditional cooperators matter mainly once the threshold is passed, to keep cooperation stable rather than to start it", "A few unconditional cooperators may be needed to reach the threshold, beyond which conditional cooperators can keep it going"], "a": 3, "e": "Hindmoor: successful collective action 'will not usually depend upon everyone in a group eschewing their self-interest. In its early stages it may however require that a few are prepared to do so' (p. 127). The 'most members' option overstates, the 'guaranteed to spread' option overstates (he shows limited cooperation can be stable, not that it grows to 100 percent), and the last option reverses when unconditional cooperators matter."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "except", "q": "Which statement about tit-for-tat is NOT consistent with Hindmoor's account of Axelrod's tournaments?", "o": ["It won both tournaments, judged by its average score against all the other strategies", "It cooperates in the first round and then copies what the other player did last round", "It beats every rival strategy head-to-head, including the strategy of always defecting", "Its success is explained by its simplicity, its generosity and its responsiveness to others"], "a": 2, "e": "Hindmoor stresses that 'tit-for-tat will not beat any strategy': against always-defect it loses the first round and never catches up (p. 119). It wins on average performance across all opponents, which the three accurate options describe."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "excerpt", "q": "A housing report argues: 'Every tenant in the city would gain from stricter rent rules, but each reasons that one membership fee to the tenants' union makes little difference and that any rule will benefit them anyway. The few large landlords, each with much at stake, lobby effectively.' Which reading or concept grounds this argument most directly?", "o": ["Taylor's rent-seeking: insiders capture betterment from a growth boundary and pass costs on to buyers", "Olson's logic of collective action: tenants free-ride, while a few landlords with much at stake organise", "Needham's private-law route: tenants hold property rights that they can enforce in court by themselves", "Hardin's tragedy of the commons: a shared rivalrous resource is overused by every individual user"], "a": 1, "e": "The passage describes non-excludable benefits (rent rules help all tenants) and the reasoning that one's contribution makes little difference, which is Olson's free-riding (pp. 103-104), plus the asymmetry the HC6 Airbnb slide drew between small concentrated and large diffuse groups. Taylor's rent-seeking is tempting because landlords lobby, but the passage says nothing about betterment, boundaries or shifting costs to outsiders; Hardin concerns overuse of a rivalrous resource, not failure to provide a good."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: In Taylor's sample, landowners who mentioned plans to develop their land rarely objected to the GAIC. Statement II: Owners who knew what development would earn them saw the charge as small compared with the expected gain.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 0, "e": "Both are in Taylor's analysis (Table 4, p. 271): very few owners with development plans objected, against about half of those without plans, and she explains this by their knowledge of development value, which made the charge look affordable. That is why she concludes that opposition was driven by landowners framing it as a tax on existing value, not by developers."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Taylor argues that landowners who invoked rural amenity were all lying to hide their financial motives. Statement II: Taylor holds that landowners' altruistic arguments can unavoidably line up with their property interests.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 3, "e": "Taylor explicitly does not claim landowners were lying: she tests only whether at least part of their claims was driven by perceived self-interest and accepts many argue in good faith (pp. 260, 266). Statement II is her point, close to Clingermayer's 'happenstance', where altruistic goals unintentionally align with rent-seeking ones, so I is false and II true."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: After Melbourne's 2010 boundary expansion, land prices inside the UGB rose sharply relative to comparable land outside. Statement II: For economists, whether a boundary premium stems from the supply side or the demand side matters, because it decides whether the boundary is efficient.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 1, "e": "Both statements are in Taylor (pp. 259, 262), but II does not explain I: it is about how economists interpret a premium, not about why Melbourne's prices rose. Taylor even adds that for fringe landowners the supply/demand distinction is irrelevant; what counts is simply that land outside is worth less than land inside."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "combo", "q": "Which of these does Taylor identify as ways in which submissions doubted or discredited the expected windfall from inclusion in the UGB? I. Calculating the charge as a share of the owner's current property value. II. Disbelieving, or mocking, the idea that inclusion would raise land values. III. Accepting that values might rise elsewhere, but not on their own land for some years.", "o": ["I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III", "I and III only"], "a": 2, "e": "Taylor describes all three (pp. 269-270): misunderstanding or ignoring the betterment logic (one owner concluded he would lose more than half his home), open disbelief ('who would guarantee the increase?'), and accepting gains elsewhere but not soon on one's own land. The last framing was central to the Taxed Out! campaign, so leaving it out understates her account."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality draws a new boundary for village expansion. Farmers just outside lobby to be brought inside, while owners of large villas inside lobby against extra housing to protect their views and home values. Which pairing of Taylor's concepts fits?", "o": ["Farmers: homevoters protecting amenity and value; villa owners: premium-seeking rent-seekers", "Farmers: outsiders who will bear the costs; villa owners: insiders who capture the betterment", "Farmers: dual predictability; villa owners: a hypothecated tax on betterment from the boundary", "Farmers: premium-seeking rent-seekers; villa owners: homevoters protecting amenity and value"], "a": 3, "e": "Taylor's two rent-seeking interests (p. 264): premium-seeking owners want inclusion to unlock development value, while 'homevoters' (Fischel) defend restrictions that protect amenity and property values. The insiders/outsiders option is tempting, but both groups here are existing owners, i.e. insiders; outsiders are future homebuyers."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: a farmer's land is brought inside a growth boundary and its price jumps tenfold without any effort on her part. She then lobbies, with neighbours, to have the new levy on that gain deferred until the land is developed. In Taylor's terms, the price jump and the lobbying are respectively...", "o": ["rent-seeking, an unearned increment from public action; and betterment captured by the farmer", "betterment, an unearned increment from public action; and rent-seeking to keep that increment", "dual predictability created by the boundary; and a homevoter strategy to protect local amenity", "a hypothecated tax on the increment; and premium-seeking to be excluded from the growth boundary"], "a": 1, "e": "Betterment is the rise in land value caused by public action such as rezoning, and taxing it is justified because it is unearned (p. 263); rent-seeking is using political influence to secure or keep such unearned income, as landowners did in getting the GAIC redesigned and deferred. The homevoter option fails because the farmer is not defending restrictions for amenity; she is protecting a development gain."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "critique", "q": "Commentary practice: which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Taylor's analysis fits that perspective's logic?", "o": ["Marxist: Taylor overstates conflict, since land rents are a neutral reward that efficient owners earn in a free market", "Sociological institutionalist: Taylor overstates the role of norms, since owners simply maximise their own utility", "Evolutionary (Hayekian): the boundary itself creates the scarcity rents, so the deeper fault is discretionary containment", "Communicative: Taylor ignores that open participation always yields consensus on the common good of the whole city"], "a": 2, "e": "A Moroni or Hayek-inspired critic would shift the blame from owners to the rule: a discretionary boundary creates artificial scarcity and therefore the very rents that invite capture, so general rules rather than boundaries plus taxes are preferred. The other pairings invert their logic: Marxists see land rent as socially produced value privately appropriated, sociological institutionalists would say Taylor underplays norms, and Taylor's finding is precisely that open participation amplified those with a stake."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "nuance", "q": "Which paraphrase states Taylor's central finding most precisely?", "o": ["Landowners discredited the expected windfall and on that basis escaped liability, which moved largely to purchasers", "Landowners proved that inclusion did not raise land values, so the government withdrew the infrastructure charge entirely", "Developers led the campaign against the charge and got payment deferred until after the new homes had been sold to buyers", "Landowners accepted that a windfall would occur but persuaded the government to cut the per-hectare rate of the charge"], "a": 0, "e": "Taylor (p. 273): landowners managed to discredit the idea that windfall profits would follow and so escaped formal liability; liability moved to purchasers and was later deferred to development. They did not prove values would not rise (prices soared), the charge survived, and opposition came from landowners, not developers, who tend to accept charges they can pass on."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "mechanism", "q": "Which causal chain matches Taylor's concluding picture of urban containment in Melbourne?", "o": ["Containment lowers land premiums; insiders lobby to capture or keep them; costs are passed on to homebuyers as outsiders", "Containment creates land premiums; outsiders lobby to share in them; costs are passed back to existing landowners as insiders", "Containment creates land premiums; insiders lobby to capture or keep them; costs are absorbed by developers as lower profits", "Containment creates land premiums; insiders lobby to capture or keep them; costs are passed on to homebuyers as outsiders"], "a": 3, "e": "Taylor's cycle (p. 274): containment creates price premiums in the land market, these are redistributed through the lobbying of 'insiders', and the costs are likely passed on to housing consumers as 'outsiders'. The developer option is the most tempting, but Taylor notes developers accept charges they can pass on to buyers, so the burden falls on future homebuyers."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "except", "q": "Which statement about Taylor's method and claims is NOT accurate?", "o": ["She analyses public submissions as claims, testing whether part of them reflects perceived self-interest", "She draws on Evans' insider-outsider problem: participation is biased towards existing property owners", "She uses price data to show that the 'purchaser pays' model made new homes measurably more expensive", "She notes that the GAIC's survival, even in modified form, shows that stakeholder influence has limits"], "a": 2, "e": "Taylor's study is a content analysis of 264 submissions; it does not measure who actually bore the GAIC, and she calls the 'purchaser pays' incidence an assumption (p. 272). The other three statements are accurate (pp. 260, 273)."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Rational choice institutionalism expects self-interested actors to create or change institutions deliberately. Statement II: In rational choice institutionalism, institutions provide certainty, information and credible commitment, which make cooperation possible.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 0, "e": "Both are on the rational choice institutionalism slide (after North and Sorensen 2017), and II gives the reason for I: because rules supply credible commitment and information, rational actors have an incentive to 'create rules to enhance cooperation', as Ostrom's commons users do. 'Both true, no explanation' would ignore that the function of institutions is exactly what motivates their creation in this strand."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Rational choice institutionalism holds that institutions shape what actors prefer in the first place. Statement II: Sociological institutionalism holds that institutions establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 3, "e": "In rational choice institutionalism preferences are given and institutions are external constraints and incentives that actors calculate around, so Statement I is false. Statement II paraphrases the sociological slide quote ('they establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences'), which is exactly what separates the two strands."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "assertion", "q": "Statement I: Historical institutionalism, on the lecture's map, centres on path dependency and critical junctures. Statement II: Sorensen argues that path dependence usually means complete lock-in, with no change between critical junctures.", "o": ["Both true, and II explains I", "Both true, but II does not explain I", "I is true, II is false", "I is false, II is true"], "a": 2, "e": "Statement I is the historical institutionalism slide (Hall and Taylor 1996; structural emphasis). Statement II reverses Sorensen: path dependence is 'usually not lock-in' but continuous contestation within a relatively constrained developmental pathway, which is why the lecture used him to qualify both lock-in and free design."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "combo", "q": "Which statements fit historical institutionalism as presented on the neo-institutionalism map? I. It stresses path dependency and critical junctures. II. It sees institutions as procedures, routines and norms embedded in the structure of the polity. III. It sees most institutions as the unintended outcome of slow evolution that cannot be designed like organisations.", "o": ["I and III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "a": 1, "e": "I and II come from the historical institutionalism slide (Hall and Taylor 1996), with Holsen's Norwegian participation case as example. III is Moroni's evolutionary institutionalism; history matters there too, but the claim about unintended evolution and the limits of taxis-style design belongs to the evolutionary strand."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: in a Dutch housing cooperative, any major renovation of the shared building requires a two-thirds majority of members at the general meeting. In Ostrom's IAD framework, what type of rule is this?", "o": ["An aggregation rule: it sets how individual votes combine into one collective decision", "A choice rule: it sets which actions on the building members may or may not undertake", "A boundary rule: it sets who may enter or leave the cooperative as a full voting member", "A payoff rule: it sets which rewards or sanctions follow from the outcome of the decision"], "a": 0, "e": "Aggregation rules 'determine how individual actions combine into collective decisions'; the slide's example is majority voting in a village council. A choice rule is tempting because renovations are actions, but this rule does not permit or forbid a renovation as such; it specifies the decision procedure for the group."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "case", "q": "Hypothetical: farmers along a shared Dutch polder ditch, tired of others neglecting maintenance, meet and agree on a cleaning rota, a log anyone can check, and fines for skipped turns. Which institutionalist strand explains this most directly, and why?", "o": ["Evolutionary: the rota is an unintended outcome of slow evolution that nobody consciously designed", "Sociological: farmers adopt the rota because it fits cultural templates of appropriate behaviour", "Historical: the rota follows a path set at a critical juncture long ago and is now hard to change", "Rational choice: self-interested users deliberately craft rules that make cooperation credible"], "a": 3, "e": "This is Ostrom's logic, which the lecture placed under rational choice institutionalism: self-interested users solve a commons problem by creating rules for allocation, monitoring (information) and sanctions (payoff) that overcome the 'problem of commitment'. The evolutionary option is tempting because the rules arise bottom-up, but here they are deliberately agreed, not unintended outcomes of slow evolution."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "critique", "q": "Commentary practice: the lecture shows the chain 'planners design institutions, institutions shape behaviour, behaviour reshapes society and space' (Innes, via Alexander). Which pairing of a strand with its critique of this chain fits that strand's logic?", "o": ["Sociological: design fails because actors calculate payoffs and pay no attention to symbols or meanings", "Historical: design is constrained by path dependence, and real openings come mainly at critical junctures", "Rational choice: institutions evolve unintentionally, so users can never craft rules for themselves", "Evolutionary: planners should engineer comprehensive new rule systems in a taxis-oriented manner"], "a": 1, "e": "Historical institutionalists qualify the design chain by path dependence: change is most likely at critical junctures and then proceeds within a bounded pathway (Sorensen). The other pairings invert their strand: sociological institutionalists stress meanings and internalised scripts, rational choice institutionalists think actors can craft rules (Ostrom), and Moroni explicitly rejects the taxis-oriented, engineering approach."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "nuance", "q": "Which paraphrase captures the lecture's summary of Dembski and Salet on institutional change most precisely?", "o": ["Symbols can be deployed by planners at will, and if they are well designed they quickly instil new meanings in residents", "Symbols are irrelevant to institutional change, which follows only from changes in formal rules such as laws and plans", "Symbols cannot be used voluntaristically; planners should seek niches of under-institutionalised space for new practices", "Symbols matter only after new institutions have been designed top-down, as a way of communicating them to the public"], "a": 2, "e": "The slide quotes: symbolic markers 'cannot be used in a voluntaristic way', new institutions emerge through new cultural practices that become institutionalised, and 'the art of planning is to find niches of underinstitutionalised space'. The 'at will' option overstates planners' control, and the 'top-down' option reverses the direction: meaning emerges in practice, it is not added after design."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "mechanism", "q": "Which causal chain correctly captures how, in the lecture's account of Ostrom, institutions overcome the tragedy of the commons?", "o": ["Each user fears others overuse; users craft monitoring and sanction rules; restraint becomes credible, so they cooperate", "Each user fears others will overuse; the state takes full ownership of the commons; users then lose any reason to overuse it", "Each user fears others will overuse; users craft monitoring and sanction rules; preferences change, so rules become unneeded", "Each user is altruistic by nature; users craft monitoring and sanction rules; restraint becomes credible, so users cooperate"], "a": 0, "e": "Ostrom's institutions solve the 'problem of commitment': rules on monitoring and sanctions make it credible that others will also hold back, so self-interested users can cooperate without privatisation or state control. The 'preferences change' chain swaps in a sociological mechanism, and the 'altruistic' chain removes the self-interest that defines the puzzle."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "who", "q": "Which author, as used in the lecture's exercise on institutional change, would most clearly endorse this claim: 'Crises open short periods in which actors' choices have a heightened chance of setting the path that later contestation follows'?", "o": ["Dembski and Salet, on symbolic markers", "Sorensen, on land value capture in Toronto", "Moroni, on an evolutionary theory of reform", "Ostrom, on governing the commons by rules"], "a": 1, "e": "Critical junctures as 'short periods' with a 'heightened probability' that agents' choices matter, and actors using 'conjunctural crises' to push change, are the Sorensen quotes on the exercise slide. Moroni is tempting because he also discusses change over time, but for him most institutions evolve unintentionally rather than being set at decisive moments."}
];
const OQ_4=[
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "short", "pts": 5, "q": "Explain what Needham (2012) means when he calls property rights a 'policy variable', and why this matters for land-use planning.", "model": "Needham argues that property rights are not natural or absolute (against Locke) but created and recognised by the state: 'It is the fact that something is protected that makes it a right' (Bromley). Because rights are created, the allocation of interests between private-law protection (rights) and public-law protection (planning) 'can always be revised', so rights can be 'deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use' (pp. 28-29). This matters because changing rights becomes an instrument of planning itself: some planning goals might be reached by creating markets in property rights, as with tradable leases on Dutch industrial estates. He adds that rights change slowly because of their ideological content and limits such as the ECHR.", "rubric": ["States that rights are created by the state, not natural (Locke/Bromley)", "Explains that the split between rights and planning protection can be revised", "Links this to using rights as a planning instrument / creating markets in rights", "Gives an example (e.g. tradable leases, covenants, future generations' trustees)", "Mentions a limit: ideological content, ECHR, slow change"]},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "excerpt", "pts": 15, "q": "Excerpt from Needham (2012, p. 29): 'It follows from this, that a discussion in terms of ‘planning or the market?’ is not useful. The market cannot be excluded from influencing land use. The only question which is interesting is about the mix of planning and the market (Buitelaar 2003). [...] So the decision whether an interest in land should be protected by private law (property rights) or by public law (planning by regulation) is a decision about the mix of planning and the market.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt or the chapter. (10)", "model": "(a) Rational choice, more precisely the institutional economics of property rights (law and economics): land use results from individuals' voluntary decisions within rules (property rights) that the state creates, and planning is one way of shaping the incentives of those decisions.\n(b) Argument 1, land use comes from voluntary, individual decisions in the market: the excerpt says 'The market cannot be excluded from influencing land use', and elsewhere Needham stresses planning 'will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions' (p. 24). Agency lies with owners who respond to rules, a typically rational choice ontology. Argument 2, institutions (property rights) structure exchange and can be redesigned: the choice between 'private law (property rights)' and 'public law (planning by regulation)' is presented as a choice of instrument, and Needham concludes that 'property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use'. This draws on Coase's reciprocity and Williamson's remediability, both economic approaches to comparing institutional arrangements, rather than on a notion of an objective public interest, which he rejects. That rejection fits the same perspective: instead of an expert-defined 'public interest' (Rydin's governmental model), planning upholds 'the identifiable interests of many identifiable private persons' (p. 26), so the unit of analysis is the individual and her interests, not society as a whole. Note the hedge that keeps this a feasibility study: markets in rights work only 'under certain limited circumstances'.", "rubric": ["(a) Names rational choice / institutional economics of property rights", "Argument 1: individual voluntary decisions and incentives, with a fitting quote", "Argument 2: property rights as designable rules framing exchange, with a fitting quote", "Explains how each quote shows the perspective, not just quoting", "Quotes are verbatim from the excerpt or chapter"]},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "apply", "pts": 10, "q": "Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality wants a new suburb's residential quality to stay high for decades but has little capacity to monitor every alteration through permits. A councillor proposes 'letting the residents arrange it themselves, as in the US'. Analyse this proposal with Needham's (2012) concepts: what would it require legally, would it work in the Netherlands, and what risks does Needham name?", "model": "In Needham's terms the residents have an interest in the amenity of their estate, now protected the public-law way: a plan, permits and monitoring by the municipality. The US alternative turns that interest into a property right: buyers enter a covenant restricting harmful changes and the right of covenanter is vested in a residents' association that monitors and sues on behalf of residents (pp. 31-32). This is 'creating a market in property rights', shifting protection from public to private law. In the Netherlands it currently would not work, because Dutch property law does not allow owners to be obliged to join a residents' association, so such covenants are not binding. The legal precondition is therefore to change a property right, which Needham says is possible because rights are a 'policy variable', but slow given their ideological content and the ECHR. He would evaluate the switch by remediability: does it reach the same goal more effectively, at what resource cost and with what distributional effects? Risks: rights-based developments can become exclusive, like gated communities, and fragment the city (p. 34). Feasibility is also not desirability.", "rubric": ["Frames the problem as an interest in amenity currently protected by public law", "Describes the covenant/residents' association solution as turning an interest into a property right", "Explains why it is not binding in the Netherlands (no compulsory association) and that rights would need to change", "Uses remediability or feasibility-not-desirability to evaluate", "Names exclusivity/gated communities/fragmentation as risks"]},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "position", "pts": 10, "q": "Place Needham (2012) on two course dimensions: structure versus agency, and reproduction versus transformation. Justify each placement with the text.", "model": "Structure versus agency: Needham leans towards agency, but agency within state-made structures. Land use results from owners' voluntary decisions: it is 'not command and control', and 'Land-use planning will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions' (p. 24). At the same time these decisions are framed by property rights that the state defines and courts enforce, so institutions structure the incentives of actors; this is the rational choice (institutional economics) mix of individual choice within rules.\nReproduction versus transformation: Needham's argument allows for transformation through deliberate redesign of rules. Because rights are created, not natural, 'property rights can be deliberately changed in order purposefully to influence land use' (pp. 28-29), for example making unexpired leases saleable so Dutch industrial estates are maintained. The lecture used this quote as the example of rational choice explaining transformation, in contrast to Taylor, where insiders reproduce their advantage. Needham qualifies this: rights carry high ideological content and change slowly, and markets in rights are feasible only 'under certain limited circumstances'.", "rubric": ["Places Needham towards agency with a quote about voluntary/private decisions", "Acknowledges the structuring role of state-created property rights", "Places him towards transformation via deliberate redesign of rights, with quote", "Notes qualifications (slow change, limited circumstances) or contrast with Taylor", "Uses the course dimensions correctly"]},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "short", "pts": 5, "q": "Explain what Hindmoor means by the 'second-order collective action problem' and give one way in which it can be overcome.", "model": "Selective incentives (rewards for contributors or punishments such as ostracism for free-riders) help groups overcome free-riding, but applying them is itself a collective good. Why would any one worker ostracise a free-riding colleague, given that the benefit of deterring free-riding goes to everyone? Explaining that by fear of being ostracised oneself leads to 'an infinite regress' (pp. 116-117). Hindmoor gives two ways out. Entrepreneurs, such as revolutionary leaders, can supply selective incentives because they expect a personal reward, for instance a job in the future regime. Alternatively, process benefits can do it: on Israeli kibbutzim shirkers become the target of gossip, and members gossip simply because gossiping is enjoyable, so no further incentive is needed.", "rubric": ["Defines selective incentives (positive/negative)", "Explains that applying them is itself a collective good (regress)", "Gives a correct solution: entrepreneurs or process benefits (kibbutz gossip)", "Explains why that solution avoids the regress"]},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "excerpt", "pts": 15, "q": "Excerpt from Hindmoor (2006, pp. 102 and 113): 'Olson (1965: 2) suggests that ‘unless the number of individuals is quite small, or there is coercion or some other special device to make individuals act in their common interest, rational, self-interested, individuals will not act to achieve their common or group interests’. [...] For all these reasons it is tempting to conclude that Olson is simply wrong and that there is no collective action problem (see Jordan and Maloney, 1996). Yet this would be to misread Olson’s argument. Olson argues that there is a collective action problem. He does not suggest that groups will always fail to overcome it.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote. (10)", "model": "(a) Rational choice theory, specifically the public choice / collective action branch: social outcomes are explained from the choices of instrumentally rational, self-interested individuals.\n(b) Argument 1, methodological individualism and self-interest as the behavioural assumption: the claim is built on 'rational, self-interested, individuals' who 'will not act to achieve their common or group interests'. A group outcome (no collective action) is explained by aggregating individual cost-benefit reasoning: each person's contribution makes little difference while the benefit is non-excludable, so free-riding is individually rational. This is the logic Hindmoor then formalises as an N-person prisoners' dilemma with a dominant strategy of defection.\nArgument 2, solutions work by changing individual incentives, not by appealing to shared values: Olson does not say groups 'will always fail', but they succeed when 'the number of individuals is quite small', or through 'coercion or some other special device'. Those devices (state coercion, selective incentives such as free legal aid, privileged members, conditional cooperation in intermediate groups) all alter the payoffs a rational individual faces. Even the response to the critics stays within this logic: the frequent success of unions or pressure groups does not refute Olson but shows that such devices are at work. Hindmoor's own assessment marks the limit of this perspective: it cannot explain people who contribute because a norm says they ought to.", "rubric": ["(a) Names rational choice / collective action (public choice)", "Argument 1: self-interested individuals and free-riding, with quote", "Argument 2: solutions as incentive-changing devices (coercion, special devices, small numbers), with quote", "Explains why the misreading ('impossible') is wrong", "Quotes verbatim from the excerpt"]},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "apply", "pts": 10, "q": "Hypothetical: the 60 households of a Dutch street want to jointly finance a neighbourhood heat network. All would gain from lower, more stable energy costs, but sign-ups stall. Analyse why, using Hindmoor's account of Olson, and propose two ways a planner could help, each linked to a solution from the chapter.", "model": "The heat network is close to a collective good for the street: if it reaches the needed number of connections, benefits such as lower collective costs, less disruption and a greener street are hard to confine to contributors, while each household bears its own costs of joining. Each may reason that its sign-up makes little difference and wait for others: a collective action problem, here partly with an assurance or threshold structure, since the network only goes ahead above a certain number of connections. Hindmoor's Figure 5.5 suggests that early on, cooperators are worse off until the threshold is passed, so starting is the hard part. Solutions: (1) Privileged member: if a housing corporation owns twenty homes in the street, it may value the network enough to commit first and carry the start-up costs, letting others follow. (2) Selective incentives: a lower connection fee or tariff available only to early joiners, offered by the municipality acting as an entrepreneur. (3) Conditional cooperation: the street is an intermediate group whose long-term residents can see who has signed up; publishing a sign-up tally lowers monitoring costs (as in Valencia) and lets people cooperate once others do. Group size is only a tendency: a close-knit street can organise.", "rubric": ["Identifies the collective action / free-riding problem with the reason (contribution makes little difference)", "Notes the threshold or assurance aspect, or the role of early cooperators", "Two solutions correctly linked to the chapter (privileged group, selective incentives, conditional cooperation, coercion, entrepreneurs)", "Uses the Dutch setting concretely", "Mentions group size as only a tendency or similar nuance"]},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "compare", "pts": 10, "q": "Compare Hindmoor's account of Olson with sociological institutionalism (neo-institutionalism lecture) on one specific point: why people contribute to collective goods.", "model": "For Olson, as Hindmoor presents him, individuals are self-interested and treat contributing as 'a cost to be minimized'. They contribute only when incentives make it pay: coercion, selective incentives such as free legal aid, being a privileged member, process benefits, or conditional cooperation in groups where free-riders can be seen and sanctioned. Preferences are given; institutions such as Ostrom's monitoring rules matter because they change payoffs and make commitment credible, which is the rational choice institutionalist reading. Sociological institutionalism starts elsewhere: institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences'. People contribute because a norm or cognitive script defines what is appropriate and is internalised (logic of appropriateness, goodness-of-fit with legitimate templates), not because they calculate. Hindmoor himself admits this limit: many people contribute 'because a social norm leads them to believe that they ought to', and Olson concedes his theory cannot explain 'non-economic' groups like Greenpeace or Amnesty. Hindmoor also calls for social psychology on group identity. The difference is thus ontological: given self-interest versus socially constructed preferences. Hindmoor's Box 5.5 sits in between, arguing that people may follow norms but often manipulate or drop them when self-interest conflicts.", "rubric": ["Olson: self-interest, contribution as cost, incentive-based solutions", "SI: institutions shape preferences, norms internalised, appropriateness", "Uses Hindmoor's own limit (norms, Greenpeace/Amnesty) as bridge", "Names the underlying difference (given vs constructed preferences)", "Stays on the specified point with concrete references"]},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "short", "pts": 5, "q": "Explain Taylor's (2016) distinction between 'insiders' and 'outsiders' and why it matters for value capture at the urban fringe.", "model": "Taylor borrows Evans' insider-outsider problem: planning participation is biased towards existing property owners (insiders), while future residents and homebuyers (outsiders) have no voice. In Melbourne, insiders used the public inquiry to discredit the expected windfall from the UGB expansion and to get the GAIC redesigned: small parcels exempted, liability shifted to purchasers, and payment deferred to development. Because developers accept charges they can pass on, the cost is likely to land on future homebuyers. This matters because value capture aims to make owners share an unearned gain created by public action; insider lobbying can instead turn it into a charge on outsiders, worsening affordability.", "rubric": ["Defines insiders (existing owners) and outsiders (future buyers/residents)", "Links to biased participation (Evans) or rent-seeking", "Uses the GAIC example (purchaser pays, deferral, exemptions)", "Explains the consequence for value capture / affordability"]},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "excerpt", "pts": 15, "q": "An article on a planned urban extension in a Dutch region argues: 'Once the province announced the new urban contour, offers for farmland just inside the line multiplied. When the municipality proposed a levy on the value increase, owners' associations claimed that their land had not gained in value and that the eventual homebuyers should pay instead. The council adopted a deferred levy, payable by the developer at the building stage. In planning, the scarcity created by a boundary produces gains that existing owners organise to keep.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt. (10)", "model": "(a) Rational choice, specifically public choice theory of rent-seeking, as in Taylor's (2016) analysis of Melbourne's growth boundary and the GAIC.\n(b) Argument 1, actors respond strategically to the incentives a rule creates: 'offers for farmland just inside the line multiplied' shows that the boundary creates a scarcity premium (Taylor's 'dual predictability': clear where development may go and that such land is scarce), and buyers and owners act on it. The final sentence makes the general claim explicit: 'the scarcity created by a boundary produces gains that existing owners organise to keep'. The explanation runs from self-interested behaviour within rules, not from norms or meanings.\nArgument 2, rent-seeking by insiders shifting costs to outsiders: owners 'claimed that their land had not gained in value and that the eventual homebuyers should pay instead', and 'The council adopted a deferred levy, payable by the developer at the building stage'. This mirrors Taylor's finding that landowners discredited betterment and won 'purchaser pays' and deferral; because developers pass charges on, the burden falls on future buyers. Lobbying to keep an unearned gain created by public action is rent-seeking, and the organised advantage of a concentrated group also echoes Olson's logic of collective action. A sociological reading would instead stress how owners' moral claims about fairness and years of hard work gained legitimacy; the passage, however, explains the outcome from interests and incentives.", "rubric": ["(a) Names rational choice / public choice (rent-seeking), possibly with Taylor", "Argument 1: incentives created by the boundary (scarcity/dual predictability) with quote", "Argument 2: rent-seeking, insiders shifting costs to outsiders, with quote", "Explains the link between each quote and the perspective", "Quotes are verbatim from the excerpt"]},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "compare", "pts": 10, "q": "Compare Taylor (2016) with Sorensen (2024) on one specific point: how they explain the fate of land value capture instruments.", "model": "Both study land value capture (LVC): public recovery of land-value increases created by public action. Taylor explains the fate of Melbourne's GAIC through rational choice, specifically rent-seeking: in one short policy episode, existing landowners ('insiders') used public submissions to discredit the expected windfall and pushed the charge towards 'purchaser pays' and deferral, so that 'policies are decided in large part on the basis of whether or not they survive conflict with groups with an existing economic stake'. The explanatory unit is self-interested actors and their lobbying. Sorensen explains Toronto's LVC with historical institutionalism: over decades, critical junctures opened by housing crises (the 1946 Planning Act, the 1989 Development Charges Act, Bills 108 and 23) set developmental pathways, after which contestation continued within a bounded range ('path dependence is usually not lock-in'). Interest-group pressure matters in both, but for Sorensen it works through timing, permissive and productive conditions, and inherited institutions, while for Taylor it is the direct strategic behaviour of owners. Taylor's case could be read by Sorensen as one moment of reactive contestation within a pathway; Sorensen's case shows why similar lobbying can enhance or cut LVC depending on the juncture.", "rubric": ["Identifies the shared topic (LVC / betterment capture)", "Taylor: rent-seeking by insiders in one episode, with detail", "Sorensen: critical junctures, path dependence without lock-in, long time span", "States the contrast in explanatory logic (actors' interests vs institutional history)", "Stays focused on the specified point"]},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "apply", "pts": 10, "q": "Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality plans a large urban extension on farmland and wants landowners to contribute to new infrastructure from the value increase. Using Taylor (2016), predict how different landowners will respond and explain what the municipality should take into account in the timing and design of the contribution.", "model": "Taylor predicts two kinds of rent-seeking. Premium-seeking owners, typically farmers or owners of vacant land at the edge, will lobby to be included in the extension to unlock development value; owners outside may call any exclusion unfair, like Melbourne's grassland owners. Homevoter-type owners, such as residents of rural-residential properties, may oppose the extension to protect amenity and value stability, often using sincere arguments about landscape that unavoidably align with their property interests. Once a contribution is proposed, owners are likely to dispute that any betterment exists, frame it as a tax on existing value or home equity, and argue that developers or buyers should pay, since developers accept transparent charges they can pass on. The municipality should therefore expect participation to be dominated by insiders and to underrepresent future residents, and recognise that concessions such as deferral or 'purchaser pays' may shift costs to outsiders and hurt affordability. In design terms, Taylor's case suggests clarity about the basis of the charge (a share of planning-created value, not existing value) and fixing the rules before the boundary decision raises expectations, because the betterment appears as soon as the line is known (dual predictability). These design points are inferences from Taylor's findings, not her explicit recommendations.", "rubric": ["Predicts premium-seeking and homevoter responses correctly", "Predicts discrediting of betterment and 'purchaser pays' arguments", "Discusses insider bias and cost-shifting to outsiders/affordability", "Gives timing/design points grounded in dual predictability or the GAIC story", "Distinguishes Taylor's findings from own inferences"]},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "short", "pts": 5, "q": "Explain what Savini (2019) means by calling institutions 'ontologically both the context and object of planning practices', and why this matters for planners.", "model": "Institutions are the formal and informal rules of the game (laws, procedures, norms of appropriateness), not organisations. As context, they are the framework within which planning decisions are made: land planning law or norms about participation constrain what planners can do. As object, they are also what planning produces: plans, new rules and arrangements are institutions that then frame later decisions. The lecture calls this the 'double life' of institutions: they constrain actors but are also outcomes of human action. This matters because it makes institutional change part of planning itself (Alexander: 'institutional transformation must be a critical aspect of planning') while raising the question of how far planners can deliberately reshape the rules that shape them.", "rubric": ["Defines institutions as rules/norms, not organisations", "Explains 'context' with an example", "Explains 'object' with an example", "Links to the double life / planning as institutional change and its limits"]},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "excerpt", "pts": 15, "q": "An article on Dutch housing policy argues: 'The tax deduction for mortgage interest was introduced when few households owned their homes. Each year it remained in place, more households planned their finances around it and more interests became attached to it, which made reform ever more costly for politicians. Only the financial crisis opened a short window in which reform became possible, and even then the change that followed was gradual and stayed within the logic of the existing system.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a quote from the excerpt. (10)", "model": "(a) Historical institutionalism, a strand of new institutionalism (Hall and Taylor 1996; in the course Sorensen and Holsen).\n(b) Argument 1, path dependency through self-reinforcing rules: 'Each year it remained in place, more households planned their finances around it and more interests became attached to it, which made reform ever more costly for politicians.' An institution created under earlier conditions persists and becomes harder to change because actors adapt to it, which is the path dependency the lecture illustrated with the growing cost of the US mortgage interest deduction. The emphasis is structural: the rule shapes later choices rather than being freely chosen each time.\nArgument 2, critical junctures followed by bounded change: 'Only the financial crisis opened a short window in which reform became possible', which matches the definition of a critical juncture as a short period in which agents' choices have a heightened chance of affecting outcomes, opened by a crisis (Sorensen's permissive conditions). And 'the change that followed was gradual and stayed within the logic of the existing system' fits Sorensen's point that path dependence is usually not lock-in but contestation within a constrained developmental pathway. A rational choice institutionalist would focus on actors' cost-benefit calculations, and a sociological one on norms of homeownership; this passage instead explains the outcome through timing, sequence and institutional legacy.", "rubric": ["(a) Names historical institutionalism", "Argument 1: path dependency / self-reinforcement with a fitting quote", "Argument 2: critical juncture and bounded (non-lock-in) change with a fitting quote", "Explains how each quote shows the perspective", "Distinguishes from at least one other strand"]},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "position", "pts": 10, "q": "Place the four institutionalist strands on the lecture's map (rational choice, sociological, historical, evolutionary) on the deliberate versus emergent dimension of change, and explain what each placement implies for planning agency.", "model": "Rational choice institutionalism sits towards deliberate change: institutions are 'humanly devised constraints' that change when self-interested actors create rules to enhance cooperation (Ostrom's commons users), so planners can redesign rules and incentives, close to 'planning is institutional design' (Innes, via Alexander). Historical institutionalism is mixed: change is mostly path-dependent and emergent, but at critical junctures deliberate choices matter a great deal (Sorensen), so planners should recognise and use crises while expecting bounded change afterwards. Sociological institutionalism leans towards emergent change: institutions are internalised scripts and meanings, and symbols 'cannot be used in a voluntaristic way'; new institutions emerge as new cultural practices become normal (Dembski and Salet), so planners work through meaning and look for 'niches of underinstitutionalised space'. Evolutionary institutionalism is the most emergent: most institutions are unintended outcomes of slow evolution and cannot be designed like organisations (Moroni), so planners should intervene modestly through general, open rules (a kosmos rather than taxis approach). The lecture's conclusion ties this together: 'How we conceptualize institutions shapes how we conceptualize planning agency.'", "rubric": ["Places rational choice towards deliberate with justification", "Places historical as mixed (junctures vs path dependence)", "Places sociological towards emergent (symbols not voluntaristic, practice)", "Places evolutionary as most emergent (Moroni, kosmos vs taxis)", "Draws the implication for planning agency"]},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "apply", "pts": 10, "q": "Hypothetical: a new national law obliges Dutch municipalities to involve residents early in spatial projects. Five years later, an evaluation finds that in one municipality participation remains a formality held after the main decisions are taken. Explain this outcome from a historical and from a sociological institutionalist perspective, and indicate what each would recommend.", "model": "Historical institutionalism: the formal rule changed, but the procedures, routines and norms embedded in the municipality's organisation did not. Established ways of preparing projects (decide with developers first, consult afterwards) are path dependent: staff, budgets and relations with developers are organised around them, so the new law is absorbed into the old routine. This is the logic of Holsen's study of why participation in Norwegian land-use planning stayed weak despite decades of legislation. A historical institutionalist would recommend looking for a critical juncture, for instance a crisis or a failed project that opens a window, and using it to change the sequence of decisions, while expecting change afterwards to be gradual and bounded. Sociological institutionalism: the problem is meaning. Officials and politicians may hold cognitive scripts in which professional expertise defines the plan and participation means informing residents; the law did not change what counts as appropriate. Change requires new practices that become normal through repetition, for example pilot projects where residents co-design early, building legitimacy by goodness-of-fit with valued templates. Following Dembski and Salet, planners should look for under-institutionalised niches rather than impose new meanings voluntaristically.", "rubric": ["HI: embedded routines/path dependency explain persistence despite formal change", "HI recommendation: critical juncture, bounded change", "SI: norms/cognitive scripts/appropriateness explain the formality", "SI recommendation: new practices, symbols/meaning, niches, not voluntaristic", "Uses lecture examples (Holsen, Dembski and Salet) correctly"]}
];
const NQ_4=[
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "define", "q": "How does Needham define land-use planning in his working definition?", "o": ["Public authorities deciding through binding plans who may own which land and how each parcel must be developed", "Public authorities pursuing a politically chosen goal by deliberately influencing how land in an area is used", "Experts identifying the land use that best serves the public interest and translating it into permits", "The state allocating property rights so that markets automatically produce efficient patterns of land use"], "a": 1, "e": "Needham's working definition (p. 23): 'the activities of public authorities when they try to achieve some politically determined goal by deliberately influencing the way in which land in a certain area is used'. The 'who may own' option contradicts his point that planning is not command and control, and the public-interest option is the justification he rejects."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "not", "q": "Needham lists ways in which a public authority can influence land use while the owner's decision 'remains free'. Which is NOT one of them?", "o": ["Granting or refusing a building or zoning permit", "Giving a subsidy or imposing a levy on a land use", "Building roads that make some uses more attractive", "Expropriating the land, amicably or compulsorily"], "a": 3, "e": "Permits, subsidies or levies, persuasion and infrastructure all influence a decision that remains free (pp. 24-25). Taking ownership away is different: then 'the decision ceases to be free', which Needham says is common for roads and open space."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "not", "q": "Needham uses Hodgson's conditions for when a 'market' exists. Which is NOT one of those conditions?", "o": ["One person has something another person wants", "Exchange is voluntary, with free choice of partner", "Prices are set or approved by a public authority", "Such exchange takes place regularly between people"], "a": 2, "e": "Hodgson's three conditions (p. 29) are a wanted good, voluntary exchange with free choice of supplier and buyer, and regular exchange between more people. Price-setting by an authority is no part of the definition, which is why Needham can treat most voluntary land-use decisions as made 'in the market'."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "example", "q": "Which example does Needham give of an interest in land that is protected neither by a property right nor by a public authority?", "o": ["My interest in the colour of my neighbour's front door", "My interest in not having my house foundations undermined", "My interest in clean air near a polluting factory site", "My interest in keeping the landscape around my town open"], "a": 0, "e": "The bright pink door (p. 28): it has been decided that my interest in its colour is less important than hers, so it gets no protection at all, a political choice. Foundations are protected by a property right (right of support), clean air by public law, and open landscape by regional planning or tools like conservation agreements."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "inverse", "q": "Needham illustrates that a property right is a way of using land 'recognized by, and protected by, the courts'. Which example does he use?", "o": ["A neighbour digging a deep hole can be sued for damaging the foundations of the house next to her garden", "An owner renting land to a farmer cannot evict him to sell to a developer if the farmer's right bars it", "A factory near housing is refused a building permit because its emissions would harm hundreds of residents", "A trust buys a farmer's development rights and never uses them, so that the open landscape is preserved"], "a": 1, "e": "The farmer-tenant example (p. 27) shows that the courts will not recognise the owner's action against a right he has granted himself, which 'undermines yet further the idea that property rights are or can be absolute'. The foundations example illustrates the right of support and the private-law route, not the definition of a right as such."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "define", "q": "Needham quotes Coase: 'Should A be allowed to harm B or should B be allowed to harm A?' What point does this make?", "o": ["Harm is reciprocal: restricting A to protect B harms A, so rights involve a choice between them", "Harm is one-sided: A causes it and B suffers, so the only question left is how strongly to restrain A", "Harm should be settled by the courts alone, because public authorities lack the legitimacy to judge it", "Harm can be ignored when total benefits are large, so efficiency always outweighs fairness in planning"], "a": 0, "e": "Coase's point of reciprocity (p. 30): 'To avoid the harm to B would be to inflict harm on A', so deciding whether to elevate an interest into a right should rest on its effects. The 'one-sided' option is exactly the common view Coase calls wrong."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "example", "q": "How does Needham illustrate that the interests of future generations in non-replaceable resources could be protected by private law instead of public restrictions?", "o": ["Have the municipality impose stricter permit conditions on the use of scarce resources for future people", "Let current owners trade their use rights freely, since the market will price in the needs of the future", "Ask today's residents to sign covenants that a residents' association enforces for future generations", "Vest those interests as property rights in a future generation, with trustees protecting them until then"], "a": 3, "e": "Following Bromley, Needham suggests rights 'vested in the generation which will be 21 in ... 50 years' with trustees appointed to protect them (p. 29). The permit option is the usual public-law route he contrasts this with."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "contrast", "q": "How do Marx and Needham differ on private property rights and the position of poorer people?", "o": ["Marx: give workers more rights in land; Needham: abolish rights in land so that planning can steer it fully", "Marx: property rights are natural and fixed; Needham: they are a neutral tool that has no political effects", "Marx: abolish private property to end class rule; Needham: empower the poor by giving them rights in land", "Marx: property rights do not matter for history; Needham: they are what mainly drives the class struggle"], "a": 2, "e": "Marx locates the motor of history in the means of production and calls for abolishing private property; Needham ends with the 'rather old-fashioned' idea that poorer people's power can be strengthened by giving them rights, quoting Blomley: 'the Left should re-appropriate property rights' (pp. 34-35). He says rights-based approaches are neither more right nor more left, not that they are politically neutral in effect."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Which advantages for the planning agency does Needham expect if planning goals could be reached through markets in property rights?", "o": ["More direct control over outcomes, more tax revenue, and faster approval of all permit applications", "Less direct involvement, less financial risk, and less risk of freezing land use in an unsuitable state", "Fewer lawsuits from citizens, no need for land-use plans, and full public ownership of the land", "Lower salaries for planners, no compensation claims at all, and guaranteed public support for plans"], "a": 1, "e": "Needham (p. 24) lists for the agency: 'less direct involvement, less financial risk, and less risk of freezing land use in a state which turns out to be inappropriate'. For citizens he adds more freedom, protection against the powerful, and more responsibility for their surroundings."},
{"t": "needham", "fmt": "apply", "q": "The lecture slide on Needham shows the 'dual role of the state'. In that diagram, who are the 'target groups' of public policies?", "o": ["Non-owners, whose interests the state protects by enforcing the rights of the owners", "End-beneficiaries, who receive the gains from policies that restrict the owners' rights", "Owners, whose behaviour the state modifies with instruments in order to benefit others", "Courts, which the state instructs to enforce public policies against private developers"], "a": 2, "e": "The slide (HC6) shows the state enforcing owners' property rights against non-owners and, in its second role, using instruments to modify the behaviour of target groups (owners) for end-beneficiaries. That is why owners' choices limit what planning can achieve, echoing 'planning will succeed only if it can influence the private decisions'."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "define", "q": "In Box 5.1 (Hindmoor), how is a toll good such as a toll bridge classified?", "o": ["Non-excludable but rivalrous", "Non-excludable and non-rivalrous", "Excludable but non-rivalrous", "Excludable and also rivalrous"], "a": 2, "e": "Toll goods can exclude non-payers but one user does not reduce what others get (Box 5.1). Common-pool goods like fisheries are non-excludable but rivalrous; public goods like defence are neither; private goods like chocolate bars are both excludable and rivalrous."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "example", "q": "How does Hindmoor use Olson against pluralists such as Truman and Bentley?", "o": ["Latent groups like migrant workers may never form, although they share vital common interests", "Pressure groups always form around shared interests, so they reflect public views well", "Large groups organise more easily than small ones, because they have more resources", "Interest groups form only when the state coerces people into joining an association"], "a": 0, "e": "Pluralists assume every set of people sharing an interest forms a pressure group, so groups represent public views; Olson replies that 'latent' groups like migrant workers may not form despite 'vital common interests' (p. 104). The 'always form' option is the pluralist assumption he attacks."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Why, according to Gambetta as discussed by Hindmoor, did the Mafia prosper in nineteenth-century Southern Italy?", "o": ["It offered process benefits, since members enjoyed taking part in its activities", "It was trusted to enforce market exchanges where the state was weak and mistrusted", "It formed a privileged group in which one family bore the full cost of public order", "It owned most of the land, so it could act as a single investor-landlord in the region"], "a": 1, "e": "Gambetta's Mafia played the role of a coercive leviathan: it promised revenge on anyone who acted dishonestly, in return for a share of each exchange, which lowered transaction costs where the state could not (pp. 113-115). It is an example of coercion by a non-state actor."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "inverse", "q": "Which example illustrates how entrepreneurs can solve the second-order collective action problem?", "o": ["Kibbutz members gossip about shirkers, because gossiping is enjoyable in itself for those involved", "A flatmate who hates squalor cleans the whole flat alone, while the others simply let her do so", "Two farmers repair a dam that will only hold if both of them work on it for the whole day", "Revolutionary leaders promise rewards to joiners and enmity to free-riders, hoping for jobs later"], "a": 3, "e": "Hindmoor (p. 117): entrepreneurs such as revolutionary leaders offer selective incentives and may themselves be motivated by 'an attractive job in a future revolutionary regime', avoiding the infinite regress. Kibbutz gossip also addresses a second-order problem, but through process benefits, not entrepreneurship."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "not", "q": "Which is NOT one of the conditions under which, according to Hindmoor's reading of Ostrom, conditional cooperation is most likely to emerge?", "o": ["Members expect to interact with each other for a long time", "Members can detect cheaply whether others cooperated", "Members can leave the group easily and at little cost", "Members form a tightly integrated, close-knit community"], "a": 2, "e": "Hindmoor lists long-term interaction, groups that are difficult or costly to leave (kibbutzim), cheap detection of past cooperation (Valencia) and tightly integrated groups (pp. 120-121). Easy exit undermines cooperation, because free-riders need not fear future sanctions."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "example", "q": "Why did commanders in the First World War start rotating their troops, according to the trench warfare example?", "o": ["To spread the burden of fighting fairly, so that no unit could free-ride on the others", "To erode the trust behind informal cooperation, which depends on long-term interaction", "To give soldiers process benefits, since moving between sectors kept their morale high", "To create a privileged group of elite units willing to bear the full cost of an attack"], "a": 1, "e": "Soldiers facing each other for long periods evolved norms like firing only at set times, even a Christmas football match; commanders rotated troops 'knowing that this would erode trust' (p. 120). It shows that conditional cooperation needs stable membership and repeated interaction."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "recall", "q": "What did Marwell and Ames find in their experiment with an 'individual' and a 'group' bank, as reported by Hindmoor?", "o": ["Many invested in the group bank; when private returns rose, contributions fell but stayed positive", "Nearly everyone invested only in the individual bank, exactly as the self-interest model predicts", "Contributions to the group bank rose steadily as the payoff from the individual bank was increased", "Players gave to the group bank only when they could punish free-riders after every single round"], "a": 0, "e": "In a game equivalent to a one-shot prisoners' dilemma, self-interested players should invest nothing in the group bank, yet a substantial share did; raising the individual payoff from two to five dollars cut group investment from about 40 to about 20 percent, but not to zero (pp. 123-125). This supports the limits of the self-interest assumption."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "apply", "q": "The HC6 lecture applied Olson to short-term rentals (Airbnb). Why, on that slide, does regulation lag despite collective harm?", "o": ["Neighbours organise well because they are many, but platforms hold property rights that courts protect", "Hosts free-ride on each other's lobbying, while neighbours form a privileged group that pays everything", "Regulators lack information about rentals, so the problem is one of knowledge rather than collective action", "Small, concentrated hosts and investors organise well, while large, diffuse groups of neighbours free-ride"], "a": 3, "e": "The slide states Olson's insight: 'small, concentrated interests (hosts/investors) organize effectively', while 'large groups struggle to mobilize due to free riding', so 'regulation lags, despite collective harm'. Hindmoor would add that group size is only an empirical tendency."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "contrast", "q": "How do Hardin and free-market economists differ in their solution to the tragedy of the commons (Box 5.3)?", "o": ["Hardin: privatise the commons; free-market: let a coercive state leviathan control access to the commons", "Hardin: users craft their own monitoring rules; free-market: the state sets quotas and fines for overuse", "Hardin: a coercive leviathan controls access; free-market: privatise, create rights or tradeable permits", "Hardin: rely on norms of restraint; free-market: rely on users' altruism and gossip about overgrazing"], "a": 2, "e": "Hardin argues people must be made responsive to 'a coercive force outside their individual psyches, a leviathan', so the state should control access; free-market economists (Demsetz) blame missing property rights and propose privatisation and, for pollution, tradeable permits based on the Coase Theorem (p. 114). Self-organised rules are Ostrom's third way."},
{"t": "olson", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Which question does Hindmoor say Olson's book does NOT answer, so that a comprehensive theory of collective action would still need it?", "o": ["Why free-riding arises when benefits are non-excludable", "How selective incentives help groups overcome free-riding", "Why large groups tend to be latent more often than small", "How individuals come to identify with a particular group"], "a": 3, "e": "Olson does not 'provide a comprehensive theory of collective action': he cannot say how individuals acquire interests that make them identify with a group, how platforms are agreed, how collective action changes policy, or why groups collapse (p. 128). Free-riding, selective incentives and group size are exactly what the book does address."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "define", "q": "Taylor calls the GAIC in effect a 'hypothecated' tax. What does 'hypothecated' mean here?", "o": ["Its rate depends on a hypothetical future value set by the land's owner", "It is charged only after the land has been sold to a final homebuyer", "It replaces existing property taxes on farmland outside the boundary", "Its revenue is earmarked for one use: infrastructure in growth areas"], "a": 3, "e": "A hypothecated tax has its revenue earmarked for a specific use; the GAIC funded infrastructure in new growth areas (pp. 259-261). Charging only at final sale is not what the term means, and the original design was in fact payable at sale or subdivision."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "define", "q": "In Taylor (2016), what is 'betterment'?", "o": ["Improvements an owner makes to land, such as drainage, buildings or new access roads", "A rise in land value caused by public action, such as moving a boundary or rezoning", "The share of a land-value rise that a developer passes on to the final homebuyers", "The extra amenity that lifestyle owners enjoy when growth restrictions are kept in place"], "a": 1, "e": "Betterment, also called development gain, windfall or planning gain, is the unearned increase in land value that arises largely from community or government action (p. 263). That it is unearned is the political case for taxing it, as the GAIC tried to do."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "contrast", "q": "Taylor borrows two routes to rent-seeking from Clingermayer. How do 'heresthetics' and 'happenstance' differ?", "o": ["Heresthetics: altruistic aims unintentionally align with rents; happenstance: deliberate manipulation of an issue", "Heresthetics: lobbying by developers; happenstance: lobbying by farmers who happen to own land at the urban fringe", "Heresthetics: deliberate manipulation of an issue; happenstance: altruistic aims unintentionally align with rents", "Heresthetics: rent-seeking through the courts; happenstance: rent-seeking through public inquiry submissions"], "a": 2, "e": "Heresthetics is the intentional manipulation of an issue; happenstance is when altruistic goals unintentionally line up with rent-seeking ones (p. 260). This lets Taylor say owners may argue in good faith while their arguments still serve their property interests."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Why could the original GAIC design hit ordinary vendors who simply wanted to sell, not only developers?", "o": ["It was payable at sale or subdivision of the land, whichever came first", "It was charged every year on all land within twenty kilometres of the boundary", "It applied only to parcels under one acre, which are mostly owner-occupied", "It was levied on rural land outside the boundary that might be included later"], "a": 0, "e": "The GAIC was a flat, one-off charge per hectare on land over 0.41 hectares brought inside the UGB, payable 'at the sale or subdivision of the land, whichever came first' (p. 261), so a farmer selling up would pay it. This design fed the Taxed Out! campaign."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "example", "q": "According to Taylor's submissions data, which owners inside the new boundary most often wanted their land to stay OUT of the UGB?", "o": ["Broadacre farmers and owners of vacant land, who planned to sell and move away", "Owners with development plans, who feared the infrastructure charge would apply", "Owners of protected grassland in the west, who called the planning rules unfair", "Lifestyle owners and businesses such as a winery, valuing the current land use"], "a": 3, "e": "Those wanting out were mostly lifestyle owners and businesses such as a winery, valuing land use over development (p. 267). Broadacre farmers and vacant-land owners were the ones happy to be included, and the grassland owners wanted in."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "inverse", "q": "Which example from Taylor illustrates premium-seeking objection to planning restrictions?", "o": ["Hobby farmers argued for keeping green-wedge controls that protected their amenity", "Inner-city residents and green groups objected to the expansion on general grounds", "Western owners whose land was to be protected grassland called the rules unfair", "Developers accepted the charge, as long as they could pass it on to the homebuyers"], "a": 2, "e": "The 21 grassland owners described the protection as unfair and wanted the development value; Taylor reads this as premium-seeking, in stark contrast to non-landowners who supported grassland protection (p. 268). Hobby farmers defending green-wedge controls are the homevoter type."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Why, according to Taylor, did developers not lead the opposition to the GAIC?", "o": ["They were exempt from the charge, which applied only to farmers and lifestyle owners", "They tend to accept charges that are transparent and can be passed on to buyers", "They expected the boundary to be moved back, so the charge would never have to be paid", "They supported betterment taxes on principle, as a fair return on public investment"], "a": 1, "e": "Taylor concludes that opposition was driven by landowners, not developers, because developers tend to accept charges that are transparent and can be passed on to buyers (p. 271). This is also why landowners argued that developers, and thus purchasers, should pay."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "apply", "q": "In Taylor's Figure 1, a binding growth boundary splits land prices at the line into a higher urban price and a lower rural price. What does the gap between the two represent?", "o": ["The betterment that is unlocked for any parcel that the boundary is moved to include", "The infrastructure cost per hectare that the government must recover from owners", "The amenity value that homevoters lose when the boundary is pushed outwards", "The share of the land price that is passed on to buyers through the GAIC charge"], "a": 0, "e": "Without a boundary, urban and rural land prices would meet at one point; a binding boundary splits them, and the gap is the betterment that moving the boundary unlocks (p. 263). That gap is what the GAIC tried to tax and what premium-seeking owners lobby to obtain."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "not", "q": "Which was NOT among the changes made to the GAIC after landowner pressure?", "o": ["An exemption for sales of parcels between 0.41 and 5 hectares", "Formal liability moved in most cases to purchasers or developers", "Payment fully deferred until development, decided in 2011", "A cut of about half in the per-hectare rate of the charge"], "a": 3, "e": "The redrafted bill exempted sales of 0.41 to 5 hectare parcels (about 56 percent of liable parcels), moved liability to purchasers (30 percent at purchase, the rest at development), and in June 2011 payment was fully deferred to development (pp. 272-273). Taylor does not report a rate cut."},
{"t": "taylor", "fmt": "contrast", "q": "How does Taylor compare Melbourne with the United Kingdom regarding landowner pressure on planning?", "o": ["In Melbourne speculation usually overrides preservation, but in both places outcomes follow existing owners", "In Melbourne preservation interests usually override speculation, while in the UK owners have no effect on outcomes", "In both places planners resist owner pressure, but only the UK manages to tax development gains successfully", "In Melbourne owners act as outsiders, while in the UK the future homebuyers are the insiders who capture gains"], "a": 0, "e": "Taylor notes that in Melbourne, unlike the UK, property speculation generally overrides preservationist interests, but in both cases outcomes follow the pressure of existing owners (p. 274). The point supports her general claim that policies survive or fail depending on conflict with groups that have an existing economic stake."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "define", "q": "In the lecture, Ostrom's institutions are said to overcome 'the problem of commitment'. What does that mean?", "o": ["Rules make it credible that others will also hold back, so restraint is no longer a losing move", "Rules force users to commit their land to the state, which then manages the shared resource", "Rules let users commit to one shared set of values, so that self-interest no longer plays a role", "Rules commit planners to a single long-term plan, which they cannot later change or abandon"], "a": 0, "e": "In a commons each user fears that holding back only leaves more for others; Ostrom's self-organised rules (monitoring, sanctions) make others' restraint credible. That is a rational choice institutionalist mechanism, not state ownership or a change of values."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "recall", "q": "Following Sorensen (2017), on which three questions do the institutionalisms disagree, according to the lecture?", "o": ["Who owns land, who plans it, and who profits from it", "Which laws, which plans and which organisations matter", "How large, how formal and how old institutions should be", "What institutions are, what they do, and how they change"], "a": 3, "e": "The slide lists three questions: what institutions are, what they do, and how they transform, including their origins and whether they can be designed. The strands' answers then imply different views of planning agency."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "example", "q": "What do Tennekes, Harbers and Buitelaar (2015) show, as used in the lecture?", "o": ["Symbols linking a shipyard past to a creative future enabled a new development agenda in a city", "Different institutional arrangements produce different residential morphologies in three nearby regions", "Public participation in land-use planning stayed weak despite decades of reforming legislation", "Users of a fishery crafted their own rules on gear, catches and fines to prevent overexploitation"], "a": 1, "e": "They compare the Netherlands, Flanders and North Rhine-Westphalia and link institutional arrangements to the morphology of residential development (planned Dutch estates versus Flemish ribbon development). The other options are Savini and Dembski (Amsterdam-Noord), Holsen (Norway) and Ostrom's fishery."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "inverse", "q": "In the IAD fishing example, 'areas off-limits to fishing and protected species' illustrate which type of rule?", "o": ["Choice rule, on actions", "Boundary rule, on entry", "Scope rule, on outcomes", "Position rule, on roles"], "a": 2, "e": "Scope rules define which outcomes may be affected. Choice rules would govern allowed actions such as gear, boundary rules cover membership of the cooperative, and position rules define the available roles."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "not", "q": "Which is NOT a feature of sociological institutionalism on the lecture's map?", "o": ["Rules as external constraints that actors calculate around", "Goodness-of-fit with templates seen as legitimate", "Institutions as symbol systems, cognitive scripts and moral templates", "An internalised, rather than external, understanding of rules"], "a": 0, "e": "Treating rules as external constraints that self-interested actors calculate around is the rational choice view. Goodness-of-fit (DiMaggio and Powell), symbol systems and cognitive scripts (Hall and Taylor), internalisation and hegemonic norms are all on the sociological slide."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "define", "q": "What does 'goodness-of-fit' (DiMaggio and Powell) mean in sociological institutionalism?", "o": ["Rules survive because they give the best economic payoff to the actors who follow them", "Arrangements are reproduced because they fit established templates seen as legitimate", "Institutions change at critical junctures when they no longer fit their economic context", "Plans succeed when they fit the formal legal framework that was set by higher governments"], "a": 1, "e": "Goodness-of-fit refers to reproducing arrangements that are seen as legitimate because they match established templates. The payoff option is a rational choice logic, and the critical-juncture option is historical institutionalism."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "apply", "q": "The lecture shows a chart in which the US revenue loss from deducting mortgage interest keeps growing over the years. Which institutionalist idea does it illustrate?", "o": ["Goodness-of-fit: a rule survives because it matches cultural templates of a good home", "Credible commitment: a rule lets lenders trust borrowers, so that it lowers transaction costs", "Path dependence: an established rule keeps reproducing itself and becomes harder to change", "Spontaneous order: a rule emerges unintentionally from the decentralised choices of owners"], "a": 2, "e": "The chart sits on the historical institutionalism slide: an established rule keeps reproducing itself and grows more costly to reform, a path-dependent dynamic, linked to the 'housing is becoming unaffordable' homework. A sociological reading of homeownership norms is possible, but that is not what the chart was used to show."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "contrast", "q": "How do rational choice and sociological institutionalism differ on where actors' preferences come from?", "o": ["Rational choice: institutions shape preferences; sociological: preferences are given and rules shape incentives", "Rational choice: preferences come from class position; sociological: preferences come from critical junctures", "Rational choice: preferences evolve unintentionally; sociological: planners design preferences deliberately", "Rational choice: preferences are given and rules shape incentives; sociological: institutions shape preferences"], "a": 3, "e": "Rational choice institutionalism takes interests as given and sees rules as 'humanly devised constraints' providing certainty and credible commitment; sociological institutionalism holds that institutions 'establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences'. The second option simply swaps the two."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "recall", "q": "In Ostrom's IAD framework as shown in the lecture, what is the overall sequence?", "o": ["Exogenous variables, including rules, shape an action arena whose interactions produce outcomes that are evaluated", "An action arena produces rules, which then shape exogenous variables that are finally judged by evaluative criteria", "Evaluative criteria set the rules, which create an action arena that then directly determines biophysical conditions", "Participants first choose outcomes, then design an action arena, and only at the end agree on rules to support it"], "a": 0, "e": "In the IAD diagram, biophysical conditions, attributes of the community and rules shape the action arena (action situations and participants); interactions produce outcomes judged by evaluative criteria, with feedback to the exogenous variables. The seven rule types specify the 'rules' box."},
{"t": "ni", "fmt": "define", "q": "According to the lecture, how does new institutionalism relate to the structure-agency debate (Giddens)?", "o": ["It sides with structure: rules fully determine what actors do, leaving no room for choice", "It sides with agency: actors choose freely, and rules are just the sum of individual choices", "It reconciles them: actors make choices, but within rules that both constrain and enable", "It avoids the debate: institutions are studied only as organisations, not as rules or norms"], "a": 2, "e": "The slide presents new institutionalism as a way of answering and reconciling the relationship between structure and agency: choices sit where structure (rules) and agency (freedom) overlap. Institutions constrain, but they are also outcomes of action, their 'double life'."}
];
